=== (E) are these witnesses HARD instances of (G+k) k=2 (path >= rad+4)? ===
    A geodesic is an induced path, so path(G) >= diam(G)+1 ALWAYS.
    Hence path >= rad+4 is at risk ONLY when diam+1 < rad+4, i.e. diam <= rad+2.
    k=2 L=5: rad=6 diam=12  path >= 13  vs rad+4 = 10   -> trivially satisfied (diam > rad+2)
    k=2 L=30: rad=19 diam=37  path >= 38  vs rad+4 = 23   -> trivially satisfied (diam > rad+2)
    k=4 L=10: rad=22 diam=43  path >= 44  vs rad+4 = 26   -> trivially satisfied (diam > rad+2)

    CONCLUSION (E): the (Q-RAD) witnesses settle NON-EMPTINESS of
    {C4-free, l>4, rad>=5} but every one of them has diam >> rad+2, so none is a
    hard instance of (G+k) k=2.  The refined open fork is (Q-RAD'):
    does there exist a C4-free graph with l>4, rad>=5 AND diam <= rad+2 ?

FAILURES: 0
